 climate neutral continent in the  world by 2050  and i hope in december the european  council will agree  on our proposal for reducing emissions  by at least 55 by 2030  as compared to 1990 levels  and we can already see that our ambition  is not in isolation  china has now decided to become co2  neutral by 2060.  south africa has announced climate  neutrality by 2050.  japan and south korea have followed suit  and of course we look forward to the  united states rejoining the paris  agreement  as president-elect joe biden has  committed to  so in the months ahead we must all work  to raise the climate ambitions of other  countries  too we must first of all engage with the  economies of the g20  they are responsible for 80  of global greenhouse gas emissions  so next year the world will come  together in glasgow  for club 26 on climate  and the world will come together in  kunming  for cup 15 on biodiversity  these will be landmark moments in the  world's ability  to push back against climate change and  biodiversity destruction  and europe will be at the forefront of  brokering  ambitious commitments and i believe the  united states  is also well placed to support us i mean  given the remarkable work  going on in different american states  and cities and companies  and in civil society these events  will also be important moments for our  multilateral rules-based system  and the institutions that underpin them  we want to work with our partners to  strengthen them  be it in the united nations be it in the  who or the wto  we need strong institutions  backed by strong commitment from member  countries  our international system has been held  hostage for too  many years now so the time has come to  reverse the trend  to reform those institutions that need  reform  and to revive multilateral deals that  are essential for  common security to create new coalitions  on the most  pressing issues of our times  one such coalition is needed on the  digital world  this is an area where i believe  europe can take the initiative and  partner up with the us and others  we need to define a rule book  on the digital economy and society  covering everything from big data  to big tech from data used to data  privacy but also from infrastructure to  security  and i will just name a few fields  where this is particularly important  the first one is our relationship with  online platforms  online platforms have gained a huge  influence and economic power  and their behavior has an impact not  only on free and fair competition among  businesses  but also on our democracies  on our security and on the quality of  our information  and this immense power requires  adequate rules our values need to be  protected  online as well as offline and this means  at its most basic that what is  illegal offline should also be illegal  online  and should be pursued just as  effectively  and take illegal hate speech and  terrorist propaganda  in recent weeks we have seen once again  how urgent this  is we will propose a broad reform  with the digital services act it will  reach beyond our targeted proposal on  terrorist content and create  stronger rules for the removal of  all illegal content while protecting  free speech this is a very fine balance  and it will clarify the responsibilities  of online platforms  so to know their business customers  to be more transparent on how content  spreads and to show  how they respond to this  in parallel we are also proposing  a digital markets act  and this will set out tools to address  timely the economic power of gatekeeper  platforms  these gate keeper platforms will we have  to make sure that we protect fair  competition  and innovation and these will be the  rules for operating  in europe but it will be important  that and here i come back to the global  topic that our global partners  also raise their standards so the  european way on digital services and  markets  can be a model for others confronted by  the same challenges  and so it will become an important part  of our diplomacy  the second topic in the digital part is  for me data protection  from brazil to south korea our european  rules on personal data protection have  inspired  others to modernize their own privacy  rules  we must now put special focus  on the international transfer of data  particularly after the recent ruling of  the  european court of justice  and the third topic is a fair taxation  of the digital economy  many large digital companies are  emerging from the crisis  more profitable and with a larger market  share than ever before  well this is okay anyone who does  business in the single market  and thus benefits from our  infrastructure  from our education system or our social  system  is welcomed to make profits  but our social contract expects them  to pay appropriate taxes in order to  contribute  to this social market economy  and it cannot be that commercial giants  benefit enormously from our single  market  but failed to pay taxes where they  should  i think this undermines the acceptance  of the social market economy  and we will not longer tolerate this  and this creates an even greater urgency  to find an international agreement  on the taxation of digital businesses  and on global minimum taxation  our goal remains a consensus-based  solution at the oecd and g20 level  on both pillars of the global  discussions  but let there be no doubt  should an agreement fall short of a fair  tax  system europe will act  and the new deadline of mid  2021 must be and will be  the final one so should an arrangement  and an agreement fall short of a fair  tax system that provides long-term  sustainable revenues  we will come forward with our own  proposal  i think it is a matter of fairness  everyone must contribute their fair  share and particularly those  who are benefiting from this crisis  in other words the digital economy  should serve everyone  not just a privileged few and for this  we need global rules and international  cooperation  and europe must be the leading force  towards international cooperation on  these digital issues  ladies and gentlemen so this was a  bouquet  health climate digital reform of the  multilateral rules based systems  these are some of the indeed key areas  where i see europe can take the  initiative  and offer a positive a new agenda with  the united states  and again as we know we cannot  turn the clock back not on trade  not on ttip and we cannot go back to the  exact same agenda we had five years ago  we should not fall into that trap we  need a fresh approach  also because the world has changed and  so have the united states and  so has europe our  focus should be on providing  joint leadership to address the global  challenges of today  without being nostalgic for the world of  yesterday  and there is a lot more work we can do  on our bilateral trade issues on  standards on taxation  and many other areas we can make quick  progress  together with the administration and the  new congress  and there are so many compelling reasons  for the european union and the united  states  the two largest polls of free market  activity in the world  to work together  that is what we will do and at the same  time  we will continue to work on all our  partnerships whether they are easy  or they are complicated so take china  the eu-china relations are of  real strategic importance to both sides  and to the world  but this relationship is also one of our  most challenging and you have heard me  say  many times china is a negotiating  partner  an economic competitor and a systemic  rival all at once we have many  interests in common for example on  climate change  but we still have an unbalanced trade  and investment partnership  and long-standing issues on fair market  access  reciprocity and over capacity  and there is no doubt that we promote  very different systems of governance  and society and we must always continue  to call out human right abuses wherever  they are the european union  is fully committed to a genuine  result-oriented engagement with china  and indeed we made some breakthroughs at  our recent meeting  but we are also realistic we need to see  concrete results if we are to conclude  glued negotiations on an investment  partnership  concrete results are necessary  the point is that europe is ready to  work on all  of its partnerships whether with  neighbors  competitors or difficult negotiators  it is important for our own interests  and for strengthening global cooperation  for the common interest  but europe can only play its  full role in the world if it is  strong internally and if it takes care  of itself  so being geopolitical as you said helga  means understanding the links between  our domestic decisions and our external  action  and we have everything at our disposal  a unique single market and social market  economy  a position as the world's first trading  superpower and the world's second  currency  so we have to keep up the momentum on  all of our priorities  look at ourselves from completing the  banking union and capital markets union  to strengthening our industry to doing  more to strengthen our defence and  security and you name it  it means being assertive in defense of  fairness  for example whether through a carbon  board adjustment mechanism this will be  a big topic  or digital levy as we just discussed  the stronger more assertive we are  internally  the more we can achieve in the world and  that  is how geopolitical power is built  and nowhere will this internal strength  be more important  than in building a better world after  the crisis  i spoke about our global response to the  pandemic  now i would like to conclude about our  recovery  and how you can support it in your daily  work  around the world  over the next two years  governments around the world will spend  around  10 trillion euros for the recovery  and this is money that  we are borrowing from future generations  so we borrow now on capital markets  and we will have to give back in the  coming decades  but the choices we make today will  define the next generation's lives  our cherishes can and will shape the  economy that the next generation will  inherit  and for this reason our recovery plan  is called next generation eu and it's a  huge responsibility  we want to use it not only to recover  but to come out of the crisis with a  better economy  it has to be greener it has to be more  digital  and it has to be more resilient and thus  fit for the next generation  europe has chosen to invest 37  of next generation eu in projects  with a positive impact on the european  green deal  and we commit to invest the rest  only in projects that do no  significant harm  we will invest another 20 percent of  next generation  eu on digital projects on infrastructure  technology artificial intelligence data  and we will ensure the recovery is fair  for all  and makes our economies more resilient  to external shocks  this is a must ladies and gentlemen  to achieve all this we will need  allies and partners all across the world  and we need to draw on our strength to  make that happen  a united europe is a diplomatic power  that can make a difference on the  international stage  we have a clear understanding the new u  s  administration but also many countries  around the world  we all have a clear understanding that  global cooperation  needs to restart it is up to  us to take the initiative and we have  the best and most experienced team  to guide us through these uncharted  waters  i think for example and want to thank  our high representative vice president  joseph burrell  for his state's craft his statesmanship  and his diplomatic knowledge all these  skills will continue to be invaluable as  we enter this next chapter in global  affairs  his experience his skills  and his drive and they are impressive  and i also want to take this opportunity  to pay tribute to helga schmidt  she has helped shape the eas from  the ground up and she has been  the authority on eu foreign policy  for well well over a decade  from javier solana to joseph burrell  her advice has been precious  and her negotiating skills are an  inspiration to manny legendary  so she does not need luck in the next  chapter of her career  but i know you will join me in wishing  her  all the best so as she will  no doubt tell you wherever you are  posted  this is a moment for action  it is a moment to reach out and make  clear that europe is ready to engage  engage for recovery engage for  resilience  engage for reforms indeed  europe is ready long live europe  thank you  you

